\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Construction process and symmetry structure.} \emph  {Top row (a--d):} Construction of a Magic Gem from the Lo Shu square. (a) The Lo Shu magic square---the unique (up to symmetry) $3 \times 3$ magic square, with each row, column, and diagonal summing to $M(3) = 15$. (b) Placement on a centered coordinate grid with origin at center. (c) Assignment of $z$-values from cell entries. (d) Centered Magic Gem point cloud with convex hull. \emph  {Bottom rows (e--l):} The eight $D_4$ symmetries of the $3 \times 3$ magic square, comprising four rotations and four reflections. Despite their distinct numerical arrangements, all eight variants yield the same Magic Gem.}}{5}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:construction}{{1}{5}{\textbf {Construction process and symmetry structure.} \emph {Top row (a--d):} Construction of a Magic Gem from the Lo Shu square. (a) The Lo Shu magic square---the unique (up to symmetry) $3 \times 3$ magic square, with each row, column, and diagonal summing to $M(3) = 15$. (b) Placement on a centered coordinate grid with origin at center. (c) Assignment of $z$-values from cell entries. (d) Centered Magic Gem point cloud with convex hull. \emph {Bottom rows (e--l):} The eight $D_4$ symmetries of the $3 \times 3$ magic square, comprising four rotations and four reflections. Despite their distinct numerical arrangements, all eight variants yield the same Magic Gem}{figure.1}{}}
